SUMMARY:Igor Siddiqui | Contingent Communities lecture
DESCRIPTION:Siddiqui's work—manifested as objects\, images\, texts\, an
 d events—links design experimentation to public engagement. He is inter
 ested in the role that interiors play in architectural experiments while 
 advocating for experimentation in interiors as a distinct field of knowle
 dge.Siddiqui's bookOblique Experiments: Claude Parent's Architectural Ins
 tallations (1969–1975)is one of the first English-language monographs o
 n French avant-garde architect Claude Parent and the first comprehensive 
 study of his experimental interior installations from the 1970s. He recen
 tly editedInterior Experiments\, a special triple-issue ofInteriors: Desi
 gn/Architecture/Culture\, where he has served as Editor-in-Chief\, Co-Edi
 tor\, and Associate Editor since 2016.His creative work has been exhibite
 d at venues including the Tallinn Architecture Biennale\, Contemporary Au
 stin\, SITE Santa Fe\, and Fusebox Festival\, and featured inDwell\,Inter
 ior Design\,Artforum\, andTexas Architect. In 2022\, Siddiqui received th
 e IIDA Educator of the Year Award. He previously taught at the University
  of Pennsylvania\, California College of the Arts\, and Parsons. Siddiqui
  is a registered architect in Texas.Learn more at:https://www.igorsiddiqu
 i.com@isssstudio on InstagramThe Eskenazi School's Comprehensive Design a
 nd Interior Design programs bring renowned designers to campus for public
  lectures and studio visits as part of the Design Speaker Series\, genero
 usly supported with a gift from Reed Bobrick and Steve Tuchman.
