SUMMARY:Osamu James Nakagawa: TRACE | Exhibition
DESCRIPTION:Opening reception and artist talk: Friday\, September 4\, 5�
 �8 pm  Nakagawa combines photographs taken at the sites of Japanese-Ameri
 can incarceration camps with photograms of found objects and rubbings of 
 the memorials.  Using diptychs\, Nakagawa pairs the past and the present\
 , flattening the boundaries between them. TRACE seeks to reveal what can'
 t be seen anymore\, things that happened eighty years ago and have been q
 uietly buried. Through a direct and immersive process\, using actual obje
 cts and surfaces from the sites\, Nakagawa unearths this history of force
 d incarceration.About the artist:  Osamu James Nakagawa was born in New Y
 ork City in 1962\, raised in Tokyo\, and then returned to the US at the a
 ge of 15. He is the Distinguished Professor and the Ruth N. Halls Profess
 or of Photography at Indiana University. Nakagawa is a recipient of the 2
 010 Guggenheim Fellowship\, the 2010 Higashikawa New Photographer of the 
 Year and 2015 Sagamihara Photographer of the Year in Japan.  Nakagawa's w
 ork has been exhibited internationally\, selected solo exhibitions includ
 e PGI\, Tokyo\; Kyoto University of the Arts\; Sepia EYE\, New York\; Sak
 ima Art Museum\, Okinawa\, Japan\; and others. Group shows include "A Sha
 red Elegy" with Emmet Gowin and Elijah Gowin\; and "After Photoshop" at T
 he Metropolitan Museum of Art\, New York. His work is included in numerou
 s public collections\, including George Eastman Museum and the Tokyo Phot
 ographic Art Museum.  Link to the artist's website:https://jamesnakagawa.
 com/en (https://jamesnakagawa.com/en)  FAR Center for Contemporary Arts:h
 ttps://thefar.org/events/event/121689 (https://thefar.org/events/event/12
 1689)
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p><strong>Opening reception and artist talk
 : Friday\, September 4\, 5–8 pm</strong></p><p>  Nakagawa combines phot
 ographs taken at the sites of Japanese-American incarceration camps with 
 photograms of found objects and rubbings of the memorials.</p><p>  Using 
 diptychs\, Nakagawa pairs the past and the present\, flattening the bound
 aries between them. TRACE seeks to reveal what can't be seen anymore\, th
 ings that happened eighty years ago and have been quietly buried. Through
  a direct and immersive process\, using actual objects and surfaces from 
 the sites\, Nakagawa unearths this history of forced incarceration.</p><p
 ><strong>About the artist:</strong></p><p>  Osamu James Nakagawa was born
  in New York City in 1962\, raised in Tokyo\, and then returned to the US
  at the age of 15. He is the Distinguished Professor and the Ruth N. Hall
 s Professor of Photography at Indiana University. Nakagawa is a recipient
  of the 2010 Guggenheim Fellowship\, the 2010 Higashikawa New Photographe
 r of the Year and 2015 Sagamihara Photographer of the Year in Japan.</p><
 p>  Nakagawa's work has been exhibited internationally\, selected solo ex
 hibitions include PGI\, Tokyo\; Kyoto University of the Arts\; Sepia EYE\
 , New York\; Sakima Art Museum\, Okinawa\, Japan\; and others. Group show
 s include "A Shared Elegy" with Emmet Gowin and Elijah Gowin\; and "After
  Photoshop" at The Metropolitan Museum of Art\, New York. His work is inc
 luded in numerous public collections\, including George Eastman Museum an
 d the Tokyo Photographic Art Museum.</p><p>  Link to the artist's website
 :<br /><a href="https://jamesnakagawa.com/en">https://jamesnakagawa.com/e
 n</a></p><p>  FAR Center for Contemporary Arts:<br /><a href="https://the
