LOCATION:The Republic Building
GEO:39.199929;-85.921713
SUMMARY:Frances Lightbound: The Land Dissolves in Water | Lecture
DESCRIPTION:Lecture: October 27\, 6:30–8pmExhibition: October 27–Dece
 mber 6\, 2026\, Mon.–Thur. 10am–4pm when IU classes are in session  L
 ightbound studies the production of space in urban environments\, weaving
  narratives across photography\, print\, spatial interventions\, and scul
 pture\, with a focus on materials and sites in transition.  Architecture 
 becomes an entry-point to questions of land use\, ownership and access\, 
 bodily experience and perception\, and timeframes ranging from the human 
 to the geological. Lightbound's talk will tease out loose threads in seem
 ingly fixed systems of spatial organization\, inviting viewers to conside
 r and participate in the continual (re)construction of meaning in urban e
 nvironments.  Frances Lightbound holds a B.A. from Glasgow School of Art\
 , Scotland\, and an M.F.A. from the School of the Art Institute of Chicag
 o. Recent solo exhibitions include Louise Hopkins Center for the Arts (Lu
 bbock\, TX)\, RULE Gallery (Marfa\, TX)\, and the John David Mooney Found
 ation (Chicago\, IL)\; her work has been exhibited internationally at ven
 ues in London\, Glasgow and Berlin. Lightbound is a Lecturer at School of
  the Art Institute of Chicago in the departments of Print media and Photo
 graphy.The J. Irwin Miller Architecture Program Lecture and Exhibition Se
 ries brings prominent working artists and architects to Columbus. Lecture
 s take place in the auditorium and exhibitions in the gallery\, both loca
 ted within The Republic Building\, 333 Second Street in Columbus. The gal
 lery is open Monday through Friday from 9 a.m. to 5 p.m. All events are f
 ree and open to the public.
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p class="p1"><strong>Lecture:</strong> Octo
 ber 27\, 6:30–8pm</p><p class="p1"><strong>Exhibition:</strong> October
  27–December 6\, 2026\, Mon.–Thur. 10am–4pm when IU classes are in 
 session</p><p class="p1">  Lightbound studies the production of space in 
 urban environments\, weaving narratives across photography\, print\, spat
 ial interventions\, and sculpture\, with a focus on materials and sites i
 n transition.</p><p class="p1">  Architecture becomes an entry-point to q
 uestions of land use\, ownership and access\, bodily experience and perce
 ption\, and timeframes ranging from the human to the geological. Lightbou
 nd's talk will tease out loose threads in seemingly fixed systems of spat
 ial organization\, inviting viewers to consider and participate in the co
 ntinual (re)construction of meaning in urban environments.</p><p class="p
 1">  Frances Lightbound holds a B.A. from Glasgow School of Art\, Scotlan
 d\, and an M.F.A. from the School of the Art Institute of Chicago. Recent
  solo exhibitions include Louise Hopkins Center for the Arts (Lubbock\, T
 X)\, RULE Gallery (Marfa\, TX)\, and the John David Mooney Foundation (Ch
 icago\, IL)\; her work has been exhibited internationally at venues in Lo
 ndon\, Glasgow and Berlin. Lightbound is a Lecturer at School of the Art 
 Institute of Chicago in the departments of Print media and Photography.</
 p><p class="p1"><em>The J. Irwin Miller Architecture Program Lecture and 
 Exhibition Series brings prominent working artists and architects to Colu
 mbus. Lectures take place in the auditorium and exhibitions in the galler
 y\, both located within The Republic Building\, 333 Second Street in Colu
 mbus. The gallery is open Monday through Friday from 9 a.m. to 5 p.m. All
  events are free and open to the public.</em></p>
